{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026112006094756e6fc35d7f445b7","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The classpath captured BEFORE I wrote cc4_budget_runner.clj? No — the classpath is paths, not a file list; `test` dir IS on the classpath... the issue: the runner file is in the CURRENT worktree's test/ dir, and the child java runs with cwd = worktree → `test/yang/cc4_budget_runner.clj` exists → loadable. \"Could not locate\" — hmm, maybe the classpath string got mangled (the `\\\"` quoting in my bash -c: `\"$(cat ...)\"` — I wrote `\\\\\"$(cat target/cc4-classpath.log)\\\\\"` in Clojure string = literal `\\\"` in the bash command → bash sees `\\\"...\\\"` = escaped quotes → argument includes literal quotes?? In bash, `\\\"` is a literal `\"` character — so the -cp arg becomes `\"path...\"` WITH quote chars → invalid classpath. Fix: use plain quotes in the bash -c string: `java -Xss64m -cp \"$(cat target/cc4-classpath.log)\" clojure.main ...` — in the Clojure string just embed `\"` directly (no escape needed inside the triple-quoted python → Clojure source needs `\"` escaped as `\\\"` for the READER... I'm writing Clojure source: `(str \"exec java -Xss64m -cp \\\"\" ...)` — the `\\\"` in Clojure source = one `\"` char in the string → bash receives `-cp \"$(cat ...)\"` ✓ correct. But my python wrote `\\\\\"` → Clojure source had `\\\\\"` = backslash+quote in the STRING → bash got `\\\"` literal. So remove the extra backslash — in the Clojure source string I need the two characters `\"` around $(cat) — written in Clojure source as `\\\"`. My python string had `\\\\\\\\\"` producing Clojure-source `\\\\\"` (string contains `\\\"`) — wrong. Rewrite with a Q-sentinel for the quote.","signature":"272fe530185b47dfa2c90e38"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8