, it couldn't have: the first successful post-B/post-S runs (target/cc4-post-B.log) — those ran with the runner requiring yang.clojure-callsite-test? Let me check the timeline: the runner rewrite (clean, requiring cohort) ran post-B/post-S successfully BEFORE I built the corpus. Then corpus+EX came later. So darkstar post8 used COHORT + runner v2; jupiter post8 used CORPUS + runner v3. The corpus should be equivalent (fidelity probe)... but the probe compared only ids/roots/statuses, NOT sizes/payloads!\n\nSo there IS a corpus-vs-cohort payload difference for SOME member (probably :macro — its `rows`/`:expansion-log`?? The cohort's macro entry has :expansion-log; my corpus also has it ✓...). The storage drop from 1,118,350 → 7,277 means the corpus run DIDN'T produce the big payloads. What's big in the cohort run: lower-unit's record (rows+relation+record of a 256-application tree with full provenance ≈ 178KB ✓ matches per-artifact 178,036). In the corpus at post8 (EX=none) lower-unit IS included... and jupiter post8-S per-artifact = 1,173?!\n\nUnless — the corpus's lower-unit COMPILATION FAILED differently... no, post8 digests MATCH darkstar's post8 (f129cdc5) — the IMAGES are identical! So the compiled members are the same. But the STORAGE differs — storage sums UNIQUE side payloads. darkstar post8-S storage 1,118,350 ≈ 178,036 + smaller ones... jupiter 7,277. Same payload set, different sizes? OR — the payload SET differs: `(into [rows relation record] (:published-payloads artifact))` — the corpus's published members have :published-payloads ✓ same...\n\nWAIT. Maybe it's the runner VERSION: runner v2 (cohort) computed `payloads` for ALL annotated members including... let me diff the two runner versions' payload logic — identical (I didn't change it). Hmm... \n\nOK — actually, WAIT: look at darkstar post8-S more carefully: `:per-artifact 178036` — versus jupiter post8-S `:per-artifact 1173`. If BOTH ran the same corpus the numbers would match. Darkstar post8 ran the COHORT (v2 runner) — so the cohort's run had a 178KB payload member. The corpus's jupiter post8 run: max 1,173. The corpus vs cohort difference for payload sizes... OH!!! I SEE IT. The cohort's `projected` uses `(encoder/project (encoder/source-envelope :cc4/replay token [ast]))` — IDENTICAL in corpus... but the RECORD materializes `rows` = the member's :rows — for the LOWER-UNIT member via cohort: rows from encoder/project ✓ same...\n\nHold on — maybe the difference is the runner: v2's pipeline materializes `record` for EVERY annotated member INCLUDING the published ones (whose trees come from published-call-site-table)... same in v3.\n\nHmm, let me just CHECK directly: rerun darkstar post8 with the CURRENT runner (corpus, EX=none) — if it gives 7,277, the difference is cohort-vs-corpus (and the fidelity probe missed something); if it gives 1,118,350, the difference is host — impossible (bytes are bytes).\n\nActually — I realize the likely culprit: **`:published-payloads` in the cohort's PUBLISHED members** — `(mapv #(jing/get store % nil) ...)` — for the corpus, `publish/publish-module!` on the PRELUDE module... no wait.\n\nLet me look at the darkstar post8-S log's per-member sizes to see WHICH member was 178KB.","signature":"cc1db64dc76c4298add125ad"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"8f6604a8-2dfb-417a-8adc-1dad2a87a0ea","timestamp":"2026-11-19T22:38:12.064Z","thinking_duration_ms":18861,"thinking_display":"updates"}
